    a: "Contributory negligence is governed by **Section 12 of the Civil Law Act 1956**.\n\n**Principle:** Where the plaintiff's own negligence contributed to the damage suffered, the court shall reduce the damages by the proportion of the plaintiff's fault.\n\n**Assessment Factors:**\n1. **Seatbelt/helmet:** Failure to wear — typically 10-25% reduction\n2. **Jaywalking:** Crossing outside designated crossing — 20-40% reduction\n3. **Intoxication:** Plaintiff under influence of alcohol/drugs — 30-50% reduction\n4. **Riding without lights:** Motorcycle/bicycle at night — 15-30% reduction\n5. **Passenger in overloaded vehicle:** 10-20% reduction\n6. **Speeding by plaintiff:** 20-40% reduction depending on excess\n\n**Apportionment is based on TWO factors:**\n- **Culpability** — degree of moral blameworthiness\n- **Causative potency** — extent to which the negligence caused the harm\n\n**Key Cases:**\n- *Abdul Hamid v Pairin [1988] 1 MLJ 442* — pedestrian contributory negligence (crossing without looking)\n- *Koh Seng Tian v Mohd Noah [1979] 2 MLJ 221* — motorcycle contributory negligence apportionment\n- *Ahmad Nordin v Yip Mun Sang [2009] 2 CLJ 681* — modern approach to seatbelt apportionment\n\n**Important:** Contributory negligence is a **partial defence** — it reduces but does not extinguish the claim."
