    a: "These are two distinct heads of damage often confused in practice:\n\n**Loss of Future Earnings (LFE):**\n- Awarded when the plaintiff has suffered a **real and assessable loss of income**\n- Calculated using the **multiplier-multiplicand method**\n- Multiplicand = net annual earnings (after EPF, work expenses; tax NOT deducted in Malaysia)\n- Multiplier = years of future loss (adjusted for contingencies, capped at retirement age 60)\n- Key case: *Lim Kok Seng v Lim Ah Lai [1992] 2 MLJ 399*\n\n**Loss of Earning Capacity (LEC):**\n- Awarded when the plaintiff is still employed but is at a **disadvantage in the labour market** due to their injuries\n- Not calculated by multiplier-multiplicand — assessed as a lump sum\n- Applicable when: plaintiff is still working, has not lost actual earnings, but is at risk of losing employment in the future\n- Typically modest (RM10,000-50,000 depending on injury severity)\n- Key case: *MBf Holdings v Houng Hai Kong [1993] 2 MLJ 516*\n\n**Critical Distinction:** LFE compensates for actual loss; LEC compensates for the risk of future loss. The two are generally **mutually exclusive** — a plaintiff cannot claim both for the same period of work life. Some courts have allowed both for different periods (e.g. LFE during recovery, LEC after returning to work)."
