    q: "How do I calculate future loss of earnings using the multiplier-multiplicand method?",
    a: "Future loss of earnings is the largest head of loss in most serious PI claims. The conventional method is **multiplicand × multiplier**.\n\n**Step 1 — Multiplicand (annual loss):**\n- Take the plaintiff's **net** annual earnings (after EPF, SOCSO, tax)\n- Use the pre-accident earnings, adjusted for likely promotions/increments if supported by evidence\n- For the self-employed, average recent years' net income from accounts and tax returns\n\n**Step 2 — Multiplier (years' purchase):**\n- Start with the remaining working years to retirement\n- **Discount** for accelerated receipt of a lump sum and the contingencies of life ('vicissitudes')\n- Malaysian courts apply conventional multipliers — these are not the full arithmetical number of years\n- Section 28A Civil Law Act 1956 sets out the framework for a living plaintiff (e.g. capped reference to age and a fixed deduction in some cases)\n\n**Step 3 — Calculation:**\n- Future loss = Multiplicand × Multiplier\n- Show every component and the arithmetic in your submissions\n\n**Worked illustration (figures only):**\n- Net annual loss (multiplicand): RM48,000\n- Multiplier: 12\n- Future loss of earnings: RM48,000 × 12 = **RM576,000**\n\n**Related heads:**\n- **Loss of earning capacity** (Smith v Manchester) where the plaintiff is working but disadvantaged\n- **Lost years** where life expectancy is reduced\n\n**Caution:** Multipliers are a matter of established practice and judicial discretion — always check the latest appellate guidance and s.28A before fixing your figure."
