    q: "What court should I file a running-down claim in, and what are the monetary limits?",
    a: "Choice of forum depends on the **amount claimed** and the nature of the claim.\n\n**Magistrates' Court:**\n- First class magistrate: civil jurisdiction up to **RM100,000**\n\n**Sessions Court:**\n- General civil jurisdiction up to **RM1,000,000**\n- **Unlimited** jurisdiction for **motor vehicle accident**, landlord-and-tenant, and distress claims (s.65 Subordinate Courts Act 1948)\n- Most running-down personal injury and fatal accident claims are filed here\n\n**High Court:**\n- Claims exceeding RM1,000,000 (other than the unlimited subordinate court categories)\n- Complex or test cases\n\n**Appeal route:**\n- Magistrates'/Sessions → High Court → Court of Appeal → Federal Court (leave required)\n\n**Practical tips:**\n- Plead the amount and forum carefully — filing in the wrong court wastes costs\n- For high-value MVA claims you may still file in the Sessions Court given its unlimited MVA jurisdiction, but consider complexity and the value of the High Court for novel points\n- Diarise appeal deadlines: 30 days for the Notice of Appeal, 60 days for the Memorandum of Appeal\n\n**Note:** Always confirm the current jurisdictional limits, which are periodically revised."
