    q: "How are dependency claims calculated in a fatal accident case?",
    a: "On a wrongful death, dependants claim **loss of support** under **s.7 Civil Law Act 1956**.\n\n**Who can claim:**\n- Spouse, children, parents, and other dependants as defined in the Act\n- Each dependant's relationship and degree of dependency must be pleaded\n\n**Calculation (multiplier-multiplicand):**\n1. **Multiplicand** — the deceased's net annual income available to dependants. The conventional deduction for the deceased's own personal/living expenses is commonly **one-third** for a married deceased (adjust on evidence).\n2. **Multiplier** — years' purchase, discounted for accelerated receipt and contingencies, with reference to the deceased's age and likely working life.\n3. **Dependency = Multiplicand × Multiplier.**\n\n**Additional recoverable items:**\n- **Bereavement** — fixed RM30,000 under s.7(3A) (spouse of deceased, or parents of an unmarried minor)\n- **Reasonable funeral expenses**\n- **Estate claim** under s.8 (pre-death pain and suffering, pre-death expenses) — avoid double recovery with the dependency claim\n\n**Worked illustration (figures only):**\n- Net annual income: RM60,000; less 1/3 personal expenses = RM40,000 multiplicand\n- Multiplier: 14\n- Dependency: RM40,000 × 14 = **RM560,000**, plus RM30,000 bereavement and funeral costs\n\n**Practical tips:**\n- Obtain Letters of Administration before commencing the estate claim\n- Gather thorough income evidence (payslips, EA forms, EPF, tax returns)\n- Where dependants are minors, the settlement requires court approval\n\n**Caution:** Deductions, multipliers and the treatment of benefits are matters of established authority — verify the current position before finalising."
