    q: "What reduction applies if my client was not wearing a seat belt or helmet?",
    a: "Failure to wear a **seat belt** or a properly fastened **crash helmet** is **contributory negligence** — even though it does not cause the accident, it increases the injury.\n\n**Graduated reductions (Froom v Butcher approach, applied in Malaysia):**\n- **Up to ~25%** where wearing the protection would have **prevented the injury entirely**\n- **~15%** where it would have **reduced the severity** of the injury\n- **Nil** where it would have **made no difference** to the injury\n\n**Key points:**\n- The **defendant bears the evidential burden** of proving the failure and its causative effect on the injury\n- Medical evidence is usually needed to show what difference the seat belt/helmet would have made\n- The reduction applies to the **whole award** of damages\n- Similar reasoning applies to a pillion rider's unfastened/absent helmet\n\n**Pleading:** The defendant must specifically plead contributory negligence and the failure to wear the restraint, with particulars.\n\n**Caution:** These percentages are conventional guidance, not fixed rules — the court assesses each case on its facts and the medical evidence."
