    q: "What is the difference between loss of future earnings and loss of earning capacity?",
    a: "These are **two distinct heads** of damage that are often confused.\n\n**Loss of Future Earnings (LFE):**\n- An **actual, continuing** loss of income that will occur after trial\n- Applies where the plaintiff **cannot work**, or works at reduced earnings, because of the injury\n- Calculated arithmetically by the **multiplier-multiplicand** method\n- Must be supported by clear evidence of the income that will be lost\n\n**Loss of Earning Capacity (Smith v Manchester award):**\n- A **lump sum** for the **handicap on the open labour market**\n- Applies where the plaintiff is **currently working at full earnings** but, because of the residual disability, would be **at a disadvantage** in finding new work if they lost the present job\n- Quantum is **impressionistic** — commonly expressed as a number of months' salary\n- Requires medical evidence of permanent residual disability and a real (not fanciful) risk of job loss\n\n**Can you claim both?**\n- Not for the same loss. If there is an actual continuing loss of earnings, claim LFE. If the plaintiff is back at full pay but vulnerable, claim a Smith v Manchester award. In some cases a small Smith v Manchester award is given in addition to a partial LFE.\n\n**Pleading:** Plead each head separately and clearly, with the basis and the supporting medical/employment evidence.\n\n**Caution:** Quantification is governed by established authority and judicial discretion — verify the current approach before submission."
